('2022-89','{{c1::Specific phobia}} has the highest lifetime prevalence among anxiety disorders in the United States.',''),
('2022-9','Impaired {{c1::rapid alternating movements}} (dysdiadochokinesia) on exam indicates {{c2::cerebellar}} dysfunction.',''),
('2022-90','Repeated apneas with hypercapnia but no respiratory effort (and morning headaches) indicate {{c1::central sleep apnea}}.',''),
('2022-91','Fever, confusion, and temporal-lobe involvement with hemorrhagic CSF suggest HSV encephalitis, treated with {{c1::acyclovir}}.',''),
('2022-92','Among antidepressants, the TCA {{c1::clomipramine}} carries the highest seizure risk in overdose.',''),
('2022-93','Making the bed to stop parental nagging is {{c1::negative reinforcement}} (removing an aversive stimulus increases behavior).',''),
('2022-94','The standard of proof in a malpractice (civil) action is {{c1::preponderance of the evidence}}.',''),
('2022-95','Adolescent fantasies with degradation/masochism are usually a {{c1::normal part of developing sexual behavior patterns}}, not pathology.',''),
('2022-96','{{c1::Advanced paternal age}} is an established risk factor for schizophrenia.',''),
('2022-97','A chronic completed MCA infarct appears on CT as a {{c1::well-demarcated hypodensity with volume loss}} in the affected territory.',''),
('2022-98','Lithium''s risk of {{c1::Ebstein anomaly}} is greatest in the first trimester, and stopping a mood stabilizer in pregnancy carries about {{c2::85%}} mood-episode recurrence.',''),
('2022-99','{{c1::Cholecystokinin}} (CCK) released after a meal signals satiation.',''),
('2023-1','In Mahler''s theory, successful separation-individuation in a toddler is marked by attainment of {{c1::object constancy}}.',''),
('2023-10','Rapidly progressive dementia with myoclonus and {{c1::periodic sharp-wave}} complexes (~1/sec) on EEG suggests {{c2::prion disease (CJD)}}.',''),
('2023-100','Genome-wide association studies most often investigate {{c1::single nucleotide polymorphisms}}.',''),
('2023-101','Preoccupation with having a serious illness despite minimal somatic symptoms and reassuring workup defines {{c1::illness anxiety disorder}}.',''),
('2023-102','{{c1::Huntington''s disease}} is inherited in an {{c2::autosomal dominant}} pattern.',''),
('2023-103','{{c1::Naltrexone}} for alcohol use disorder is contraindicated in {{c2::decompensated cirrhosis}} (and acute hepatitis) due to hepatotoxicity.',''),
('2023-104','Youth with limited resources most often receive mental health services through their {{c1::school}}.',''),
('2023-105','The most commonly reported sexual dysfunction among older men is {{c1::erectile disorder}}.',''),
('2023-106','A 12-month-old who sits, crawls, and pulls to stand but does not yet cruise or walk is {{c1::developing normally}}, warranting reassurance.',''),
('2023-107','The main limitation of case-control genetic association studies is {{c1::population stratification}}, which causes spurious associations from ancestry differences.',''),
('2023-108','{{c1::Microglia}} are the resident CNS immune cells responsible for phagocytosing debris after neuronal injury and death.',''),
('2023-109','The {{c1::Bonferroni correction}} controls for multiple comparisons by lowering the significance threshold.',''),
('2023-11','In the {{c1::collaborative care}} model, a {{c2::behavioral health care manager}} tracks patients via a registry while a psychiatrist provides consultation to primary care providers.',''),
('2023-110','CNS depression with nystagmus followed by withdrawal seizures days later suggests {{c1::benzodiazepine}} intoxication and withdrawal.',''),
('2023-111','Dysfunction of the {{c1::cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical}} circuit is most closely associated with {{c2::obsessive-compulsive disorder}}.',''),
('2023-112','Coping with rejection by focusing on how one''s work helps others exemplifies the mature defense of {{c1::altruism}}.',''),
('2023-113','Exposure and response prevention is grounded in {{c1::behaviorism}}, relying on extinction of conditioned anxiety.',''),
('2023-114','A developmentally typical 2-year-old is expected to have mastered {{c1::throwing a ball}}.',''),
('2023-115','An infant''s enjoyment of peek-a-boo demonstrates emerging {{c1::object permanence}}.',''),
('2023-116','Adult neurogenesis occurs primarily in the {{c1::dentate gyrus}} of the hippocampus.',''),
('2023-117','The cortical response to inflammation is primarily mediated by {{c1::microglia}}.',''),
('2023-118','Delirium tremens is distinguished on EEG by {{c1::excessive fast activity}} in the background rhythm, unlike the diffuse slowing of other deliria.',''),
('2023-119','The major excitatory neurotransmitter in the mammalian brain is {{c1::glutamate}}.',''),
('2023-12','{{c1::Effectiveness}} trials maximize {{c2::external validity}} (generalizability) by studying real-world patients, whereas efficacy trials prioritize internal validity.',''),
('2023-120','Adequate total sleep that is fragmented into separate episodes across the day and night suggests a {{c1::circadian rhythm sleep-wake disorder}}.',''),
('2023-121','Assessment of post-traumatic epilepsy after traumatic brain injury is best done with {{c1::conventional EEG}}.',''),
('2023-122','Pramipexole relieves restless legs syndrome by acting as a {{c1::dopamine D3 receptor agonist}}.',''),
('2023-123','Abrupt anterograde amnesia with repetitive questioning that resolves within 24 hours and leaves the patient otherwise intact defines {{c1::transient global amnesia}}.',''),
('2023-124','Prenatal {{c1::alcohol}} exposure can cause microcephaly, intellectual disability, and cerebral palsy as part of fetal alcohol spectrum disorder.',''),
('2023-125','A {{c1::middle cerebral artery}} infarct is the most common, producing contralateral face and arm weakness with cortical signs.',''),
('2023-126','Forcing antibiotics on a capacitated patient who refuses pits {{c1::autonomy}} against {{c2::beneficence}}.',''),
('2023-127','Children first grasp conservation in Piaget''s {{c1::concrete operational}} stage (roughly ages 7-11).',''),
('2023-128','Holding a session outdoors at the patient''s request breaks the {{c1::frame of treatment}} in psychodynamic psychotherapy.',''),
('2023-129','Competency to stand trial hinges on whether the defendant can {{c1::understand the proceedings and work effectively with counsel}}, not their mental state at the time of the offense.',''),
('2023-13','The antidepressant {{c1::mirtazapine}} has demonstrated efficacy in treating {{c2::akathisia}}, likely via 5-HT2 antagonism.',''),
('2023-130','The standard of proof to establish physician liability in malpractice is a {{c1::preponderance of the evidence}}.',''),
('2023-131','In Mahler''s separation-individuation, the {{c1::rapprochement}} subphase (~15-24 months) features a toddler exploring independently yet anxiously returning to check on the {{c2::mother}}.',''),
('2023-132','A {{c1::prospective cohort}} study follows exposed and unexposed groups forward in time to compare incidence of an outcome like dementia.',''),
('2023-133','{{c1::Stranger anxiety}}, marked by crying when held by an unfamiliar person, normally emerges around {{c2::8-9 months}} of age.',''),
('2023-134','In classical conditioning, the previously neutral cue that comes to elicit fear after pairing (here, the {{c1::grocery store}}) is the {{c2::conditioned stimulus}}.',''),
('2023-135','{{c1::Periodic limb movement disorder}} causes repetitive limb jerks with brief arousals during sleep, often without the patient''s awareness, fragmenting sleep.',''),
('2023-136','{{c1::Slow-wave sleep}} (N3) declines markedly with age and may be nearly or totally absent in older adults.',''),
('2023-137','{{c1::Semantic memory}} (general knowledge and vocabulary) remains relatively preserved with normal aging, unlike working and episodic memory.',''),
('2023-138','First-line treatment for postpartum psychosis/mania with evidence of sustained remission at nine months is {{c1::lithium}} (or {{c2::ECT}}).',''),
('2023-139','Panic attacks confined to a single feared stimulus (e.g., bridges) with avoidance indicate {{c1::specific phobia}}, not panic disorder.',''),
('2023-14','An awake EEG in a patient with {{c2::encephalopathy}} shows diffuse slowing with prominent {{c1::theta and delta}} waves.',''),
('2023-140','On consultation-liaison services, {{c1::universal screening}} with automatic referral above a depression-scale cutoff reduces hospital {{c2::length of stay}}.',''),
('2023-141','Decision-making capacity requires the ability to {{c1::express/communicate a choice}}, along with understanding, appreciation, and reasoning, not English fluency or family assent.',''),
('2023-142','The {{c1::precuneus}} lies on the medial parietal lobe and supports self-referential processing, episodic memory, and visuospatial imagery.',''),
('2023-143','Freud''s {{c1::latency}} stage corresponds to Erikson''s stage of {{c2::industry vs. inferiority}} (school-age, ~6-12 years).',''),
('2023-144','A therapist''s own unresolved feelings (e.g., from an alcoholic parent) distorting the treatment is {{c1::countertransference}}.',''),
('2023-145','Valproate exerts epigenetic effects by inhibiting {{c1::histone deacetylase}}, increasing histone acetylation and gene transcription.',''),
('2023-146','The most common fear among preschool-aged children facing terminal illness is {{c1::separation from caretakers}}, since they cannot yet grasp death''s permanence.',''),
('2023-147','Somatostatin inhibits release of {{c1::growth hormone}}, as well as {{c2::prolactin}} and ACTH (plus TSH).',''),
('2023-148','The {{c1::Quadruple Aim}} adds {{c2::provider experience}} (clinician well-being) to better population health, patient experience, and lower cost.',''),
('2023-149','Mature defense mechanisms include {{c1::sublimation}}, {{c2::humor}}, and suppression (also altruism and anticipation).',''),
('2023-15','Hospitalizing a suicidal patient against the parents'' wishes to protect their welfare exemplifies the ethical principle of {{c1::beneficence}}.',''),
('2023-150','Treatments with the strongest evidence for anti-suicide effects are {{c1::lithium}}, {{c2::clozapine}}, and ketamine.',''),
('2023-151','{{c1::Segregation analysis}} examines how a disorder phenotype is transmitted across generations to infer its mode of inheritance.',''),
('2023-152','Ainsworth''s {{c1::Strange Situation}} experiment assesses the quality of {{c2::attachment}} between a parent and child.',''),
('2023-153','{{c1::Localized amnesia}} is the inability to recall any events within a specific, circumscribed period of time.',''),
('2023-154','Narcolepsy type 1 (with cataplexy, sleep paralysis, hypnagogic hallucinations) results from deficiency of {{c1::orexin}} (hypocretin).',''),
('2023-155','Major depression with anhedonia, morning worsening, early awakening, weight loss, and guilt warrants the {{c1::melancholic features}} specifier.',''),
('2023-156','Tardive dyskinesia (e.g., lip smacking) from chronic antipsychotics is best treated with a VMAT2 inhibitor such as {{c1::valbenazine}}.',''),
('2023-157','Involuntary vaginal/pelvic muscle spasm with anxiety and avoidance of penetration, with normal exam, indicates {{c1::genito-pelvic pain/penetration disorder}}.',''),
('2023-158','Biofeedback teaches voluntary control over normally {{c1::involuntary autonomic nervous system}} responses (e.g., heart rate, muscle tension).',''),
('2023-159','Medicare {{c1::Part D}} provides outpatient prescription drug coverage.',''),
('2023-16','{{c1::Fluctuating}} level of consciousness is the key feature distinguishing {{c2::delirium}} from dementia, depression, or psychosis.',''),
('2023-160','The leading cause of premature death in schizophrenia is {{c1::cardiovascular disease}}, not suicide.',''),
('2023-161','Thiazide diuretics like {{c1::hydrochlorothiazide}} increase lithium levels by enhancing renal lithium reabsorption.',''),
('2023-162','The most common form of genetic sequence variation in the human genome is the {{c1::single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP)}}.',''),
('2023-163','Mutations in branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase cause {{c1::maple syrup urine disease}}, with toxic accumulation of branched-chain amino acids.',''),
('2023-164','Under the 21st Century Cures Act, {{c1::psychotherapy notes}} may be withheld from patient access (an exception to information blocking).',''),
('2023-165','{{c1::RNA-sequencing}} allows unbiased, hypothesis-free detection of novel transcripts, unlike array-based methods limited to known sequences.',''),
('2023-166','{{c1::Expressive language}} (vocabulary) is largely preserved with normal aging, while processing speed and executive function decline.',''),
('2023-167','Under the HIPAA Privacy Rule, patients may obtain most disclosures of their medical records but generally have no right to access {{c1::psychotherapy notes}}.',''),
('2023-168','Performance (nonverbal) tasks on intelligence testing produce the greatest metabolic activity in the {{c1::right parietal lobe}}.',''),
('2023-169','{{c1::Interleukin-6}} drives hepatic acute-phase protein synthesis and promotes B-cell proliferation/antibody production.',''),
('2023-17','Lesions of the {{c1::orbitofrontal}}-subcortical circuit produce {{c2::disinhibition, impulsivity, and tactlessness}}.',''),
('2023-170','The {{c1::spinothalamic tract}} carries {{c2::pain}} and temperature sensation, whereas the dorsal columns carry vibration and proprioception.',''),
('2023-171','A young adult struggling with career direction and conflict with parents reflects Erikson''s task of {{c1::identity}} vs. role confusion.',''),
('2023-172','{{c1::Iron}} deficiency is the key reversible cause associated with restless legs syndrome.',''),
('2023-173','{{c1::Nitric oxide}} directly activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, raising cGMP as a gaseous signaling molecule.',''),
('2023-174','{{c1::Validity}} is the extent to which an instrument measures what it intends to measure; {{c2::reliability}} is consistency of results.',''),
('2023-175','{{c1::REM sleep behavior disorder}}, in which dreams are acted out due to loss of REM atonia, is associated with Lewy body disorders.',''),
('2023-176','A physician-patient relationship in the ambulatory setting is typically initiated by {{c1::conducting an examination}} (providing care), not mere scheduling.',''),
('2023-177','The most prevalent compulsion in adults with OCD is {{c1::checking}}.',''),
('2023-178','{{c1::Cluster headache}}, which responds to 100% high-flow oxygen, presents with severe unilateral pain and autonomic signs like {{c2::conjunctival injection}}.',''),
('2023-179','In pregnancy, rising {{c1::estradiol}} induces {{c2::UGT1A4}} glucuronidation, lowering lamotrigine levels and risking mood relapse.',''),
('2023-18','The {{c1::arcuate fasciculus}} connects the frontal and temporal lobes (Broca''s and Wernicke''s areas); its lesion causes {{c2::conduction aphasia}}.',''),
('2023-180','Abnormal {{c1::alpha-synuclein}} metabolism underlies the dementia of Parkinson disease (and dementia with Lewy bodies).',''),
('2023-181','Mental health court participation, versus treatment as usual, is associated with {{c1::fewer arrests}} (reduced recidivism).',''),
('2023-182','Binge-purge behavior in a patient with very low body weight (BMI 16) is {{c1::anorexia nervosa, binge-eating/purging type}}, not bulimia.',''),
('2023-183','The {{c1::insula}} is the cortex buried within the lateral sulcus, processing interoception, disgust, and visceral/emotional awareness.',''),
('2023-184','When prescribing the orexin antagonist {{c1::suvorexant}}, warn patients that next-day {{c2::driving may be impaired}}.',''),
('2023-185','{{c1::Pedigree (linkage) analysis}} identifies genetic markers that cosegregate with a disease phenotype within families.',''),
('2023-186','Excessive daytime sleepiness without cataplexy plus short REM latency (~10 min) is best treated with the wake-promoting agent {{c1::modafinil}}, especially given substance use history.',''),
('2023-187','{{c1::Fluoxetine}} inhibits CYP2D6, raising donepezil levels and worsening cholinergic effects like nausea and dizziness.',''),
('2023-188','The {{c1::Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression (HAM-D)}} is clinician-administered, unlike self-report scales such as the BDI or PHQ-9.',''),
('2023-189','{{c1::Multiple sclerosis}} results from failure to inhibit apoptosis (autoreactive lymphocytes escaping programmed cell death), whereas neurodegenerative diseases show excess apoptosis.',''),
('2023-19','Combining {{c1::kava}} with a benzodiazepine can cause additive sedation, confusion, and dyskinesias due to potentiated {{c2::GABA}}ergic activity.',''),
('2023-190','{{c1::Sensitization}} is learned fear in which responding strengthens to both a harmful stimulus and subthreshold stimuli, opposite of habituation.','')
insert into flashcards (question_id, cloze_text, extra) values
('2023-191','{{c1::Psychogenic}} (functional) tremor characteristically shows {{c2::entrainment}}, changing to match the rhythm of voluntary tapping in the contralateral hand.',''),
('2023-192','The {{c1::orbitofrontal}} cortex-striatal (putamen)-thalamic circuit mediates {{c2::response inhibition}} and impulse control.',''),
('2023-193','A key psychological barrier to reporting an impaired colleague is {{c1::overidentifying}} with the impaired physician.',''),
('2023-194','MELAS, with stroke-like lesions crossing vascular territories and elevated {{c2::lactate}}, is caused by a defect in {{c1::mitochondrial DNA}} (maternally inherited).',''),
('2023-195','For a student experiencing irritability at school, an appropriate accommodation is encouraging use of {{c1::self-timeout}}.',''),
('2023-196','Narcolepsy (type 1) results from loss of {{c2::orexin/hypocretin}} neurons in the {{c1::lateral hypothalamus}}.',''),
('2023-197','In classical conditioning of panic, the {{c1::unconditioned stimulus}} is the first {{c2::panic attack}}, while the bridge becomes the conditioned stimulus.',''),
('2023-198','The decreased late-life prevalence of {{c1::personality disorders}} is attributed to normal psychosocial maturation (mellowing of traits).',''),
('2023-199','When a therapist notices erotic countertransference toward a patient, the most appropriate step is to seek {{c1::consultation}} with a colleague.',''),
('2023-2','{{c1::Synaptic pruning}} of excess connections predominates during adolescence and young adulthood, refining neural networks.',''),
('2023-20','Threatening involuntary commitment to coerce a non-dangerous patient into voluntary admission invalidates {{c1::informed consent}} through {{c2::coercion}}.',''),
('2023-200','The {{c1::septal}} nuclei are part of the limbic system.',''),
('2023-201','When a patient lacks decision-making capacity for nonemergent care, the next step is to identify a {{c1::surrogate decision-maker}}, not pursue guardianship.',''),
('2023-202','{{c1::Parvalbumin}} basket GABA interneurons synchronize cortical pyramidal cells to generate {{c2::gamma}} oscillations in the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex.',''),
('2023-203','A {{c1::nonanatomic}} sensory loss, such as a sharply demarcated {{c2::stocking}} pattern in one leg, suggests functional (psychogenic) neurological disorder.',''),
('2023-204','Event-related potentials on EEG are currently most useful for confirming {{c1::conversion disorder}}.',''),
('2023-205','Irritability in neonatal opioid abstinence syndrome is attributed to {{c1::decreased dopamine}}.',''),
('2023-206','Acute intermittent porphyria (psychosis, abdominal pain, neuropathy, autonomic dysfunction) is diagnosed by urine {{c1::porphobilinogen}}.',''),
('2023-207','Among epilepsy types, {{c1::generalized tonic-clonic}} seizures carry the highest associated suicide risk.',''),
('2023-208','Cloninger''s four temperament dimensions (harm avoidance, novelty seeking, reward dependence, persistence) are largely heritable and {{c1::universal across cultures}}.',''),
('2023-209','The most common perinatal mood presentation in women with bipolar disorder is {{c1::depressive}}.',''),
('2023-21','New SSRI use causing waxing-and-waning cognition should prompt checking {{c1::serum electrolytes}}, as SSRIs can cause {{c2::hyponatremia}} (SIADH).',''),
('2023-210','{{c1::Selective}} primary prevention targets symptom-free individuals at elevated {{c2::risk}} of developing a disorder.',''),
('2023-211','Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis (with EEG extreme delta brush) is most associated with {{c1::ovarian teratoma}}.',''),
('2023-212','Recurrent episodes of hypersomnia with hyperphagia and irritability in an adolescent suggest {{c1::Kleine-Levin syndrome}}.',''),
('2023-213','Therapeutic alliance is consistently strengthened by openly examining {{c1::negative transference}}.',''),
('2023-214','Fear of negative evaluation during presentations, with anxiety others will judge one as incompetent, indicates {{c1::social anxiety disorder}}.',''),
('2023-215','{{c1::Tyrosine hydroxylase}} is the rate-limiting enzyme of catecholamine synthesis, so its loss most impairs {{c2::dopaminergic}} neurons.',''),
('2023-216','A hypodense, well-demarcated area following a vascular territory on noncontrast CT most likely represents a {{c1::remote (old) stroke}}.',''),
('2023-217','Among antipsychotics, {{c1::aripiprazole}} carries the lowest risk of {{c2::QTc}} prolongation.',''),
('2023-218','Creating things that outlast oneself to benefit future generations reflects Erikson''s {{c1::generativity vs stagnation}} stage.',''),
('2023-219','Visual hallucinations with progressive memory loss and parkinsonism suggest Lewy body dementia; {{c1::neuropsychological testing}} best aids the differential diagnosis.',''),
('2023-22','Benzodiazepines potentiate GABA-A by increasing the {{c1::frequency}} of chloride channel opening, whereas barbiturates increase channel-opening {{c2::duration}}.',''),
('2023-220','Personally tailored activities for community-dwelling dementia patients reduce {{c1::challenging behaviors}}.',''),
('2023-221','When a stimulant like methylphenidate fails in ADHD, the next step is another stimulant class such as {{c1::dextroamphetamine}}.',''),
('2023-222','If bupropion is effective and seizures remain controlled, the psychiatrist should {{c1::continue bupropion}} despite its theoretical seizure-threshold lowering.',''),
('2023-223','Redirecting anger from a feared aggressor onto a safer target (bullied child berating a sibling) is {{c1::displacement}}.',''),
('2023-224','In a curbside (informal) consultation, liability is minimized when care remains under the {{c1::requesting provider''s}} direction, who keeps responsibility for orders.',''),
('2023-225','Vagus nerve stimulation is FDA-approved as adjunctive treatment for chronic, treatment-resistant {{c1::major depressive disorder}}.',''),
('2023-226','The neuropeptide most implicated in schizophrenia spectrum pathophysiology is {{c1::neurotensin}}.',''),
('2023-227','PET imaging with FDG measures regional {{c1::glucose metabolism}}.',''),
('2023-228','Accepting responsibility for one''s own life and finding meaning in it reflects Erikson''s {{c1::integrity vs despair}} stage.',''),
('2023-229','In relapsing multiple sclerosis, disease-modifying therapy such as {{c1::ocrelizumab}} reduces future neurological relapses.',''),
('2023-23','Meta-analyses show a modest increased risk of {{c1::suicidality}} in adolescents treated with {{c2::SSRIs}}, prompting an FDA black-box warning.',''),
('2023-230','Among observational designs, the {{c1::cohort}} study best supports inference of causality because exposure precedes outcome.',''),
('2023-231','First-line psychotherapy for adolescent anorexia nervosa is {{c1::family-based treatment}} (Maudsley).',''),
('2023-232','Noncontrast {{c1::CT}} is preferred over MRI for suspected acute {{c2::intracranial hemorrhage}}.',''),
('2023-233','In a parkinsonian gait, a {{c1::resting tremor}} predicts dopaminergic responsiveness, distinguishing Parkinson disease from atypical parkinsonism.',''),
('2023-234','For acute agitation of unknown medical/psychiatric etiology, the safest IM agent is {{c1::lorazepam}}.',''),
('2023-235','Piaget''s {{c1::preoperational}} stage is marked by the emergence of {{c2::symbolic representation}}.',''),
('2023-236','Combining the MAO inhibitor {{c1::linezolid}} with fluoxetine can precipitate {{c2::serotonin syndrome}}.',''),
('2023-237','{{c1::Pimavanserin}}, a 5-HT2A inverse agonist, treats Parkinson disease psychosis without worsening motor symptoms.',''),
('2023-238','Psychoeducation and family therapy to prevent relapse after psychiatric discharge is {{c1::tertiary}} prevention.',''),
('2023-239','Huntington disease (psychosis, mood swings, chorea, incoordination) is caused by CAG {{c1::trinucleotide repeats}} on chromosome {{c2::4}}.',''),
('2023-24','The most common comorbidity in children with autism spectrum disorder is {{c1::intellectual disability}}.',''),
('2023-240','Intention tremor, dysmetria, and dysdiadochokinesia localize a lesion to the {{c1::cerebellum}}.',''),
('2023-241','Suicidal ideation and depression in an elderly patient should be {{c1::treated}} with psychotherapy and medication, not dismissed as normal aging.',''),
('2023-242','Fibrous astrocytes primarily provide structural and metabolic support of axons in {{c1::white matter}}.',''),
('2023-243','Estrogen is neuroprotective in part by decreasing inflammatory {{c1::cytokine}} production.',''),
('2023-244','{{c1::CRISPR-Cas9}} achieves precise gene editing using a guide {{c2::RNA}} to recognize target DNA sequences.',''),
('2023-245','The 5-HT1A partial agonist {{c1::buspirone}} has reported anti-aggressive effects.',''),
('2023-246','Obesity, large neck, hypertension, and daytime fatigue point to obstructive sleep apnea, shown on polysomnography as repeated {{c1::cessation of airflow (apneas)}}.',''),
('2023-247','Erikson''s school-age stage (industry vs inferiority) centers on developing a sense of {{c1::competence}} and personal worth.',''),
('2023-248','Modest therapist {{c1::self-disclosure}} to build rapport is a boundary crossing, not a boundary violation.',''),
('2023-249','Memantine preserves cognition by acting as an {{c1::NMDA receptor antagonist}} of glutamate.',''),
('2023-25','A malpractice claim fails without a {{c1::doctor-patient relationship}}, since no {{c2::duty of care}} exists toward a general media audience.',''),
('2023-250','The {{c1::frontal-occipital (occipitofrontal) fasciculus}} links emotion with cognition and supports self-regulation.',''),
('2023-251','Experiences of discrimination and social exclusion in minority populations are linked to a higher risk of {{c1::substance-related and addictive}} disorders.',''),
('2023-252','Stuttering with sound repetitions, facial grimacing, and eye blinking that worsens under social pressure defines {{c1::childhood-onset fluency disorder}} (stuttering).',''),
('2023-253','{{c1::Response cost}} contingency management removes a positive stimulus after misbehavior, exemplified by {{c2::time-outs}} for negative behaviors.',''),
('2023-254','The {{c1::22q11.2}} microdeletion (DiGeorge/velocardiofacial syndrome) is the most common genetic abnormality associated with {{c2::psychosis}}.',''),
('2023-255','The person most likely to commit workplace violence is a {{c1::stranger}}, not a current or former employee.',''),
('2023-256','{{c1::Delirium tremens}} is the most common cause of hallucinations on the hospital medical unit.',''),
('2023-257','The {{c1::medial dorsal}} thalamic nucleus projects primarily to the {{c2::prefrontal cortex}}.',''),
('2023-258','A relative risk greater than {{c1::1.0}} (e.g., 2.0) indicates the exposure is a risk factor for the disease.',''),
('2023-259','Acute fluctuating attention and consciousness with waxing-waning agitation, even in a demented elder, indicates {{c1::delirium}} rather than worsening dementia.',''),
('2023-26','In the first month of life, infants show {{c1::preferential smiling}} to the caregiver''s voice; separation distress emerges later.',''),
('2023-260','To minimize false negatives (missed cases), a screening test should maximize {{c1::sensitivity}}.',''),
('2023-261','When an insurer denies coverage for a still-impaired psychiatric patient, the appropriate step is to continue hospitalization and {{c1::initiate an appeal}}, never falsify documentation.',''),
('2023-262','Predicting a future negative outcome (e.g., assuming a symptom heralds a panic attack) is the cognitive distortion of {{c1::fortune-telling}}.',''),
('2023-263','By age {{c1::4}}, inability to {{c2::throw a ball overhand}} is most concerning for developmental delay (the other listed skills appear later).',''),
('2023-264','An infarct in the occipital lobe causing visual field deficits localizes to the {{c1::posterior cerebral}} artery territory.',''),
('2023-265','Under HIPAA, insurers are prohibited from making {{c1::payment contingent on disclosure}} of psychotherapy notes.',''),
('2023-266','Pervasive excessive emotionality and attention-seeking are the essential features of {{c1::histrionic}} personality disorder.',''),
('2023-267','The best confirmatory test for Prader-Willi syndrome is {{c1::DNA methylation analysis}}.',''),
