('2024-19','The best predictor of later functional/adaptive outcome in autism spectrum disorder is {{c1::verbal (language) ability by age five}}.',''),
('2024-190','Number needed to treat equals 1 divided by the {{c1::absolute risk reduction}}; a 2% reduction (20% vs 18%) gives an NNT of {{c2::50}}.',''),
('2024-191','Same-sex sexual play among early adolescents is {{c1::not uncommon}} and is a normal part of sexual development.',''),
('2024-192','Maintenance of wakefulness depends on the {{c1::ascending reticular activating system}}.',''),
('2024-193','With normal aging, learning new material {{c1::takes longer}}, but {{c2::complete learning still occurs}}.',''),
('2024-194','Corticotropin-releasing hormone neurons are located in the {{c1::paraventricular nucleus}} of the hypothalamus.',''),
('2024-195','Among psychiatric disorders, {{c1::autism spectrum disorder}} has the highest monozygotic twin concordance rate.',''),
('2024-196','{{c1::Aripiprazole}} is notable for causing minimal {{c2::QTc prolongation}}, unlike ziprasidone and iloperidone.',''),
('2024-197','An inpatient who retracts suicidal ideation and is disruptive without ongoing risk should be {{c1::discharged}}, as continued hospitalization is not clinically indicated.',''),
('2024-198','The most common biological cause of preventable intellectual disability from prenatal neurotoxicity is {{c1::alcohol exposure}}.',''),
('2024-199','At resting membrane potential, the NMDA receptor is blocked by {{c1::magnesium}}.',''),
('2024-2','{{c1::Introns}} are the {{c2::non-protein-coding}} segments spliced out of pre-mRNA.',''),
('2024-20','The hallmark lab abnormality of refeeding syndrome is {{c1::hypophosphatemia}}.',''),
('2024-200','Deep brain stimulation of the {{c2::subthalamic nucleus or globus pallidus internus}} in Parkinson''s disease most improves {{c1::dyskinesia}}, while axial symptoms and cognition do not.',''),
('2024-201','Alpha rhythm on EEG is enhanced by {{c1::eye closure}} and attenuated by attention or visual fixation.',''),
('2024-202','The {{c2::AMPA}} receptor is an ionotropic receptor activated by {{c1::glutamate}}.',''),
('2024-203','Involuntarily hospitalizing a dangerous patient for their own protection reflects the ethical principle of {{c1::beneficence}}.',''),
('2024-204','{{c1::Lithium}}, originally used to treat gout, was introduced by {{c2::John Cade}} for the treatment of mania.',''),
('2024-205','The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis is developed and functioning by the {{c1::second trimester}}.',''),
('2024-206','Trauma symptoms (re-experiencing, hyperarousal, avoidance) lasting from 3 days up to 1 month are diagnosed as {{c1::acute stress disorder}}, becoming PTSD beyond {{c2::1 month}}.',''),
('2024-207','Cross-culturally, dissociative experiences most commonly represent {{c1::normal religious and ritual}} phenomena rather than pathology.',''),
('2024-208','The most focally targeted brain stimulation method is {{c1::deep brain stimulation}}.',''),
('2024-209','Ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts of harming the infant suggest postpartum OCD, not psychosis; the next step is to {{c1::obtain collateral information}}, not separate mother and infant.',''),
('2024-21','Successful insanity acquittees are often hospitalized for periods as long as the {{c1::prison sentences}} they would have served; the defense succeeds in well under 1% of cases.',''),
('2024-210','{{c1::Association studies}} test whether a particular allele occurs more frequently than expected in affected individuals within a population.',''),
('2024-211','Phencyclidine produces psychosis by acting as an antagonist at the {{c2::NMDA}} subtype of {{c1::glutamate}} receptor.',''),
('2024-212','REM sleep behavior disorder (dream enactment with punching/kicking) is treated first-line with {{c1::clonazepam}} or melatonin.',''),
('2024-213','{{c1::Ketamine}} produces dissociative euphoria and is distinctively associated with {{c2::nystagmus}}.',''),
('2024-214','In Mahler''s theory, the conflict between dependence and autonomy peaks during the {{c1::rapprochement}} subphase (around 15-24 months).',''),
('2024-215','In a fitness-for-duty evaluation, the administrative psychiatrist is {{c1::not bound by usual confidentiality}} because no treatment relationship exists.',''),
('2024-216','Assisted outpatient treatment reduces criminalization by {{c1::mandating treatment}} for seriously mentally ill individuals at high risk for {{c2::violence}}.',''),
('2024-217','The leading cause of death in persons with severe mental illness is {{c1::cardiovascular disease}}.',''),
('2024-218','The Safe Schools Initiative concluded there is {{c1::no reliable profile}} of a student who will carry out a school attack.',''),
('2024-219','{{c1::Neutrality}} is the therapist''s principle of remaining nonjudgmental about the patient''s wishes and behaviors to understand them.',''),
('2024-22','Blepharospasm (involuntary orbicularis oculi contraction) is most effectively treated with {{c1::botulinum toxin}} injections.',''),
('2024-220','Oxytocin reduces fear and stress by suppressing the {{c1::amygdala}} during emotionally charged situations.',''),
('2024-221','Targeting an at-risk subgroup (e.g., counseling for those at increased perinatal depression risk) is {{c1::selective}} prevention.',''),
('2024-222','Dopaminergic neurons of the mesocorticolimbic system originate in the {{c1::ventral tegmental area}}.',''),
('2024-223','{{c1::Diffusion tensor imaging}} can reveal white matter (axonal) injury in mild traumatic brain injury when conventional MRI is normal.',''),
('2024-224','A risk factor that increases burnout among psychiatrists is working in a {{c1::non-academic}} setting.',''),
('2024-225','{{c1::Linkage disequilibrium}} identifies population haplotypes used to infer shared gene segments between unrelated individuals.',''),
('2024-226','Restless legs syndrome is linked to {{c1::iron deficiency}}, shown by decreased serum {{c2::transferrin saturation}} (and ferritin).',''),
('2024-227','The {{c1::inferior longitudinal fasciculus}} supports both object and facial recognition by connecting occipital and temporal lobes.',''),
('2024-228','The primary limitation of crossover trials is the {{c1::carryover (remnant) effect}} of the prior treatment, mitigated by a {{c2::washout period}}.',''),
('2024-229','Early learning of a skilled motor sequence recruits the corticostriatal and {{c1::corticocerebellar}} systems.',''),
('2024-23','Spermatogenesis is driven by {{c1::FSH}} acting on Sertoli cells of the seminiferous tubules.',''),
('2024-230','Among antiseizure drugs, {{c1::topiramate}} (and carbamazepine, phenytoin) most affects other drugs via {{c2::cytochrome P450}} induction/inhibition.',''),
('2024-231','Performing a ritual act to magically negate a forbidden thought (e.g., washing cups after an aggressive impulse) is the defense of {{c1::undoing}}.',''),
('2024-232','A normal finding in older adults is {{c1::loss of vibratory sensation}} at the toes.',''),
('2024-233','{{c2::Oppositional defiant disorder}} is characterized by {{c1::vindictiveness}} and arguing with authority figures, without the rights-violations seen in conduct disorder.',''),
('2024-234','The most common cause of malpractice claims against psychiatrists is {{c1::adverse effects of prescribed medications}}.',''),
('2024-235','The 1880s American "psychopathic hospital" introduced the distinction between {{c1::acute and chronic}} mental illness into clinical practice.',''),
('2024-236','Ego-dystonic intrusive harm thoughts in postpartum OCD with partial SSRI response are best managed by {{c1::increasing the SSRI dose}}, not adding an antipsychotic.',''),
('2024-237','Leptin regulates energy homeostasis via receptors in the {{c1::hypothalamus}}.',''),
('2024-238','The most common comorbidity in a patient with an anxiety disorder is {{c1::another anxiety disorder}}.',''),
('2024-239','Herpes simplex encephalitis (temporal lobe hemorrhage, fever, seizures, CSF lymphocytosis) is treated emergently with {{c1::acyclovir}}.',''),
('2024-24','{{c1::Prosopagnosia}} (face blindness) results from disconnection of inferior temporal cortices from the visual association area.',''),
('2024-240','{{c2::Anticholinergic}} drugs like {{c1::oxybutynin}} undermine the efficacy of cholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil.',''),
('2024-241','The four processes of motivational interviewing are engaging, focusing, {{c1::evoking}}, and planning.',''),
('2024-242','The most common cause of trigeminal neuralgia is {{c1::vascular compression}} of the trigeminal nerve.',''),
('2024-243','{{c1::Capitation}} pays a provider a fixed sum per patient for a defined period, regardless of services actually rendered.',''),
('2024-244','Repeated self-induced vomiting in bulimia nervosa classically produces an {{c1::elevated serum amylase}} (salivary isoenzyme).',''),
('2024-245','When a patient refuses a recommended treatment, the appropriate first step is to {{c1::assess the patient''s decision-making capacity}}, since a dementia diagnosis alone does not negate capacity.',''),
('2024-246','In the elderly, reduced total body water raises the serum concentration of {{c1::water-soluble (hydrophilic)}} drugs by lowering their volume of distribution.',''),
('2024-247','In parent-child relational problems, {{c1::goodness of fit}} describes the match between a child''s temperament and parental expectations or caregiving style.',''),
('2024-248','Melatonin is synthesized from serotonin, which is derived from the amino acid {{c1::tryptophan}}.',''),
('2024-249','In bipolar disorder, the risk of relapse in the {{c1::postpartum}} period is roughly {{c2::three times}} higher than in nonpregnant women.',''),
('2024-25','A typically developing 24-month-old can {{c1::follow two-step instructions}}.',''),
('2024-250','An anterior cerebral artery lesion characteristically causes {{c1::contralateral leg}} weakness due to involvement of the medial motor cortex.',''),
('2024-251','In caregivers, an {{c1::idealized view of the relationship}} with the deceased is a risk factor for complicated (prolonged) grief.',''),
('2024-252','In schizophrenia, a consistently demonstrated structural change is {{c1::decreased hippocampal volume}} (along with enlarged lateral ventricles).',''),
('2024-253','A transiently elevated serum {{c1::prolactin}} level (drawn within ~20 minutes) helps distinguish a generalized tonic-clonic seizure from a psychogenic nonepileptic event.',''),
('2024-254','Accommodating a trauma survivor to avoid re-traumatization during an exam reflects the ethical principle of {{c1::nonmaleficence}}.',''),
('2024-255','Both methylphenidate and amphetamine increase synaptic dopamine primarily by {{c1::blocking the dopamine transporter (DAT)}}.',''),
('2024-256','The master circadian pacemaker is the {{c1::suprachiasmatic nucleus}}, located in the {{c2::hypothalamus}}.',''),
('2024-257','Cis-regulatory elements (e.g., promoters and enhancers) act as {{c1::mediators of transcriptional activity}} for adjacent genes on the same DNA molecule.',''),
('2024-258','When a new test agrees with an established gold standard measured at the same time, it demonstrates {{c1::concurrent validity}}.',''),
('2024-259','{{c1::Huntington disease}} is characterized by atrophy of the {{c2::caudate nucleus}}, producing chorea and a ''boxcar'' ventricle appearance.',''),
('2024-26','Asking ''why me?'' and ''this isn''t fair'' reflects the {{c1::anger}} stage of the Kubler-Ross model.',''),
('2024-260','{{c1::Validity}} is the extent to which an instrument actually measures what it claims to measure.',''),
('2024-261','REM sleep behavior disorder is diagnosed by loss of normal REM atonia, seen as {{c1::excess muscle tone}} on EMG during REM sleep.',''),
('2024-262','Postpartum ''baby blues'' is a normative state of {{c1::mild mood lability}} lasting up to {{c2::two weeks}}.',''),
('2024-263','Among antipsychotics, {{c1::risperidone}} is the most potent alpha-1-adrenergic antagonist, contributing to orthostatic hypotension.',''),
('2024-264','Constructional apraxia on clock drawing localizes to the {{c1::parietal lobe}} (especially the nondominant hemisphere).',''),
('2024-265','The lenticular (lentiform) nucleus is formed by the {{c1::putamen}} and {{c2::globus pallidus}}.',''),
('2024-266','Valproic acid acts as a histone deacetylase inhibitor, producing {{c1::hyperacetylation of the N-terminal of histones}}.',''),
('2024-267','{{c1::Dopaminergic}} neurotransmission facilitates sexual arousal, whereas serotonin tends to inhibit it.',''),
('2024-268','Cyclical physical and mood symptoms in the late luteal phase causing occasional functional impairment indicate {{c1::premenstrual syndrome}}.',''),
('2024-269','{{c1::Glycine}} is a predominantly inhibitory neurotransmitter, acting mainly in the spinal cord and brainstem.',''),
('2024-27','The {{c1::ventrolateral preoptic nucleus}} promotes sleep by sending {{c2::GABAergic}} projections that inhibit arousal centers.',''),
('2024-270','In Parkinson disease, {{c1::dopamine agonists}} (e.g., pramipexole) are the class most associated with impulse control disorders.',''),
('2024-271','The {{c1::avoidant}} attachment style is characterized by an infant paying little overt attention to the parent''s presence or return.',''),
('2024-272','Despite declines in physical and cognitive health, {{c1::sense of well-being}} often increases in older age (the paradox of aging).',''),
('2024-273','After pregnancy-related sertraline dose increases, the elevated dose should generally be {{c1::left unchanged}} at delivery if the patient is stable, adjusting only for adverse effects.',''),
('2024-274','Psychotic symptoms lasting one to six months with full medical workup negative indicate {{c1::schizophreniform disorder}}.',''),
('2024-275','Unlike the genetic code, the epigenetic code is {{c1::cell-type specific}}, allowing identical DNA to produce different cell types.',''),
('2024-276','{{c1::Prader-Willi}} syndrome is caused by a {{c2::deletion}} (paternal) on chromosome 15q11-q13.',''),
('2024-277','Patients with {{c1::Lewy body}} disease show marked antipsychotic sensitivity, developing severe extrapyramidal side effects.',''),
('2024-278','Learned helplessness theory holds that a stressor''s negative impact depends on how {{c1::controllable}} it is perceived to be.',''),
('2024-279','Beyond mental health treatment, {{c1::restriction of lethal means}} has demonstrated efficacy in lowering completed suicide rates.',''),
('2024-28','The most common comorbid psychiatric disorder in epilepsy is {{c1::major depressive disorder}}.',''),
('2024-280','{{c1::Fragile X syndrome}} is caused by an unstable {{c2::CGG trinucleotide repeat}} expansion in the FMR1 gene.','')
insert into flashcards (question_id, cloze_text, extra) values
('2024-281','Most individuals with a clinical high-risk (prodromal) syndrome who convert to first-episode psychosis do so within {{c1::0-2 years}}.',''),
('2024-282','Among perinatal factors, {{c1::maternal prenatal tobacco use}} is specifically associated with increased risk of ADHD.',''),
('2024-283','When a patient asks the physician to lie to family, the appropriate response is to tell the spouse the patient {{c1::has not given permission to discuss their care}}, preserving confidentiality without deceiving.',''),
('2024-284','The primary driver of rising U.S. incarceration since 1990 is increased use of {{c1::mandatory minimum sentences}}, not higher crime rates.',''),
('2024-285','To elicit a culturally informed chief complaint, ask the patient {{c1::how they would describe the problem to a family member or friend}}.',''),
('2024-286','Sexualized play and seductive behavior in a young child is most often a manifestation of {{c1::prior sexual abuse}}.',''),
('2024-287','Normal aging in men decreases {{c1::penile rigidity}}, while the refractory period and time to achieve erection increase.',''),
('2024-288','Estrogen-containing oral contraceptives induce glucuronidation and {{c1::decrease lamotrigine serum levels}}, often requiring a dose increase.',''),
('2024-289','Needle exchange programs are a form of {{c1::harm reduction}}, aiming to reduce consequences of use rather than use itself.',''),
('2024-29','Alcohol withdrawal in pregnancy is treated with a {{c1::benzodiazepine}} (e.g., lorazepam), as untreated withdrawal is more dangerous to the fetus.',''),
('2024-290','Abnormal spontaneous activity on needle EMG occurs in lower motor neuron/muscle disorders such as {{c1::radiculopathy, plexopathy, and polymyositis}}, not central lesions.',''),
('2024-291','Low-potency first-generation antipsychotics cause more {{c1::anticholinergic and antihistaminic}} effects, such as weight gain, blurred vision, and orthostatic hypotension.',''),
('2024-292','The personality disorders most likely to attenuate with age are {{c1::antisocial, borderline, and avoidant}}.',''),
('2024-293','The IHI Triple Aim comprises {{c1::reducing per capita cost, improving population health, and improving the individual patient experience of care}}.',''),
('2024-294','The default mode network includes the {{c1::medial prefrontal cortex, posterior cingulate, and medial posterior parietal}} regions.',''),
('2024-295','Schizophrenia risk is increased by {{c1::urban upbringing, premorbid cannabis use, and migrant status}}.',''),
('2024-296','Epigenetic regulatory mechanisms include {{c1::allelic imprinting, X-chromosome inactivation, and histone acetylation}}.',''),
('2024-297','Subacute combined degeneration of the cord can be caused or mimicked by {{c1::vitamin B12 deficiency, copper deficiency, and nitrous oxide exposure}}.',''),
('2024-298','Fetal alcohol syndrome features include {{c1::microcephaly, a thin upper lip, and a smooth (poorly developed) philtrum}}.',''),
('2024-299','Heritability is highest among {{c1::schizophrenia, autism spectrum disorder, and ADHD}}.',''),
('2024-3','Fragile X syndrome results from CGG triplet expansion that silences FMR1 via {{c1::DNA methylation}}, the key epigenetic mechanism.',''),
('2024-30','One DALY (Disability-Adjusted Life Year) equals {{c1::one lost year of healthy life}}.',''),
('2024-300','Bipolar depression monotherapies with the best evidence are {{c1::quetiapine, lurasidone, and olanzapine-fluoxetine combination}}.',''),
('2024-31','Methylation-induced {{c1::silencing of the FMR1 gene}} in fragile X syndrome is an example of an epigenetic phenomenon.',''),
('2024-32','The antidepressant most likely to worsen clozapine-associated neutropenia is {{c1::paroxetine}}.',''),
('2024-33','A {{c1::negative smoking history (nonsmoking)}} lowers the risk of severe vasomotor symptoms during perimenopause.',''),
('2024-34','By age {{c2::ten}}, a child understands that death is {{c1::permanent and irreversible}}.',''),
('2024-35','Pedigree-based linkage approaches are most powerful for identifying {{c1::rare, high-penetrance}} gene variants.',''),
('2024-36','Impulsive/affective aggression is most directly regulated by the {{c1::serotonergic}} system, with low serotonin promoting aggression.',''),
('2024-37','Searching the Internet about a patient is most appropriate when needed to {{c1::ensure safety or assess reality (e.g., test grandiose delusions)}}, not for curiosity.',''),
('2024-38','The most effective protection against EMR data breaches is {{c1::training employees to recognize threats}}, since human error causes most breaches.',''),
('2024-39','The most sensitive test for confirming myasthenia gravis is {{c1::single-fiber electromyography}}.',''),
('2024-4','A surprise test rewards studying at unpredictable times, exemplifying a {{c1::variable-interval}} reinforcement schedule, which produces steady, consistent responding.',''),
('2024-40','A radio psychiatrist disclaiming a physician-patient relationship is negating the {{c1::duty}} element required to prove malpractice.',''),
('2024-41','Compared with unipolar depression, bipolar depression more often has {{c1::abrupt onset}}, earlier age of onset, and more frequent episodes.',''),
('2024-42','People with schizophrenia have a decreased risk of {{c1::rheumatoid arthritis}}, an unexplained inverse epidemiologic association.',''),
('2024-43','In schizophrenia, {{c1::good premorbid functioning}} (with insight into decline) increases the risk of completed suicide.',''),
('2024-44','Spinocerebellar ataxias are inherited in an {{c1::autosomal dominant}} pattern, often via trinucleotide repeat expansion.',''),
('2024-45','Recruiting a spouse during a study visit risks violating {{c1::voluntarism}}, as situational pressure may coerce consent.',''),
('2024-46','Sustained conjugate upward eye deviation (oculogyric crisis) days after starting risperidone is an {{c1::acute dystonic reaction}}, treated with {{c2::anticholinergics}}.',''),
('2024-47','Diagnosing {{c1::antisocial}} personality disorder in adults requires evidence of {{c2::conduct disorder}} before age 15.',''),
('2024-48','Passively holding a fixed posture for a prolonged period (a catatonic sign) is termed {{c1::catalepsy}}.',''),
('2024-49','Antipsychotic serum levels can be increased by CYP inhibitors such as {{c1::cimetidine}} and {{c2::disulfiram}}; smoking instead lowers levels.',''),
('2024-5','After a positive single-item alcohol screen, the next step is a validated {{c1::substance use screening questionnaire}} (e.g., {{c2::AUDIT}}), not a fitness-for-duty referral.',''),
('2024-50','Cortical GABAergic interneurons originate embryonically from the {{c1::ganglionic eminences of the basal ganglia}} and migrate tangentially to the neocortex.',''),
('2024-51','The rate-limiting enzyme in dopamine synthesis is {{c1::tyrosine hydroxylase}}.',''),
('2024-52','In psychoanalysis, internal forces that block a patient from attaining insight are called {{c1::resistance}}.',''),
('2024-53','{{c1::Power}} is the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis (1 minus the type II error rate).',''),
('2024-54','Perfectionism causing chronic late documentation plus interpersonal rigidity suggests {{c1::obsessive-compulsive personality disorder}}.',''),
('2024-55','The neural tube forms from {{c1::ectoderm}} through neurulation.',''),
('2024-56','A toddler''s language development is most enhanced by parents {{c1::engaging in frequent conversation and thinking aloud}}.',''),
('2024-57','{{c1::Asymmetric onset and progression}} of motor symptoms (with levodopa response) favors idiopathic Parkinson disease over atypical parkinsonism.',''),
('2024-58','A typically developing three-year-old can {{c1::pedal a tricycle}}.',''),
('2024-59','Publicly discussing a {{c1::deceased}} patient''s treatment without next-of-kin consent violates confidentiality, which persists after death.',''),
('2024-6','The GI decontamination method with the most evidence for lithium overdose is {{c1::whole bowel irrigation}}; {{c2::activated charcoal}} does not bind lithium.',''),
('2024-60','Winnicott''s {{c1::holding environment}} fosters competence by encouraging spontaneity while protecting the child from harm.',''),
('2024-61','During pregnancy, women with bipolar disorder most often experience a {{c1::major depressive}} episode.',''),
('2024-62','{{c1::Zolpidem}} carries FDA sex-specific dosing, with lower recommended doses for {{c2::women}} due to slower clearance.',''),
('2024-63','Eszopiclone and other Z-drugs produce sedation by binding the {{c1::GABA-A}} receptor, enhancing chloride influx and neuronal inhibition.',''),
('2024-64','In postpartum thoughts of harming an infant, {{c1::ego-dystonic}} intrusive thoughts (distressing, unwanted) are reassuring, unlike ego-syntonic psychotic or infanticidal urges.',''),
('2024-65','The strongest risk factor for a depressive episode during perimenopause is a {{c1::prior history of depression}}.',''),
('2024-66','In suspected giant cell (temporal) arteritis with jaw claudication, an elevated {{c1::C-reactive protein}} (and ESR) supports the diagnosis.',''),
('2024-67','Narcolepsy type 1 (with cataplexy) is caused by loss of hypothalamic {{c1::hypocretin-1 (orexin)}}-producing neurons.',''),
('2024-68','A {{c1::case-control}} study compares affected individuals to matched unaffected controls to identify disease-associated genetic variants.',''),
('2024-69','In dementia with Lewy bodies, the cholinesterase inhibitor {{c1::rivastigmine}} improves both cognition and psychiatric symptoms like hallucinations, while antipsychotics risk severe sensitivity.',''),
('2024-7','A young child attending a parent''s funeral should be accompanied by a {{c1::familiar, trusted person}} to provide support and explanation.',''),
('2024-70','{{c1::Wernicke}} aphasia (fluent, impaired comprehension) results from lesions of the posterior {{c2::superior temporal gyrus}} of the dominant hemisphere.',''),
('2024-71','Buprenorphine acts as a functional opioid antagonist via {{c1::high affinity}} but {{c2::low intrinsic activity}} (partial agonism) at the mu-opioid receptor, displacing full agonists.',''),
('2024-72','{{c1::Carbamazepine}}, a CYP3A4 inducer, reduces the efficacy of {{c2::oral contraceptives}} by accelerating hormone metabolism.',''),
('2024-73','Postpartum blues, peaking around day 3-5 with mood lability and resolving within two weeks, is managed with reassurance and {{c1::psychoeducation}}.',''),
('2024-74','Manipulating insulin dosing to lose weight (driven by body-image concerns) is classified as {{c1::psychological factors affecting other medical conditions}}.',''),
('2024-75','The {{c1::arcuate fasciculus}} is the white matter tract connecting frontal (Broca) and temporal (Wernicke) language regions.',''),
('2024-76','Using alcohol to relieve dysphoria is {{c1::negative reinforcement}}: behavior increases because it removes an aversive state.',''),
('2024-77','Huntington disease shows {{c1::anticipation}}, with offspring having {{c2::more CAG repeats}} and earlier onset than the affected parent.',''),
('2024-78','{{c1::Declarative (explicit)}} memory for facts and events is most dependent on {{c2::medial temporal lobe}} (hippocampal) integrity.',''),
('2024-79','Xylazine, an adulterant in illicit opioids, is an {{c1::alpha-2 adrenergic agonist}} and is not reversed by naloxone.',''),
('2024-8','SSRI-induced restless legs syndrome is best treated by adding a dopamine agonist such as {{c1::pramipexole}}.',''),
('2024-80','The {{c1::retinohypothalamic tract}} carries light information from the retina to the suprachiasmatic nucleus, entraining circadian rhythm.',''),
('2024-81','For moderate-to-severe Alzheimer dementia, adding the NMDA-receptor antagonist {{c1::memantine}} (glutamate modulation) to donepezil improves attention and global functioning.',''),
('2024-82','The most consistently validated risk factor for autism spectrum disorder is {{c1::advanced parental age}}.',''),
('2024-83','In structural family therapy, restricting an interaction to one parent and the child uses {{c1::boundary making}} to strengthen subsystem boundaries.',''),
('2024-84','Chronic irritability plus frequent severe temper outbursts for over a year in a child 6-18 defines {{c1::disruptive mood dysregulation disorder}}.',''),
('2024-85','A {{c1::cohort study}} best examines how different treatments affect long-term prognosis by following groups forward over time.',''),
('2024-86','The {{c1::oral}} route undergoes the most extensive first-pass (hepatic) metabolism, reducing bioavailability.',''),
('2024-87','Chronic methadone maintenance can suppress the HPG axis, causing {{c1::hypogonadism}} with reduced testosterone and sexual dysfunction.',''),
('2024-88','Carbon monoxide poisoning damages the {{c1::globus pallidus}}, producing delayed parkinsonism and asterixis (flapping limb movements).',''),
('2024-89','Hyperventilation-provoked staring spells with eyelid flutter in a child indicate {{c1::absence seizures}} (3-Hz spike-and-wave).',''),
('2024-9','Accountable care organizations (ACOs) emphasize {{c1::early preventive intervention}} and coordinated care, shifting away from fee-for-service incentives.',''),
('2024-90','A {{c1::type I error}} (alpha) is erroneously rejecting a {{c2::true null hypothesis}} (a false positive).',''),
('2024-91','Informed consent most directly reflects the ethical principle of {{c1::autonomy}}, respecting a patient''s right to self-determination.',''),
('2024-92','A veteran startled by fireworks (similar to mortar fire) demonstrates stimulus {{c1::generalization}}, where similar stimuli evoke the conditioned response.',''),
